# Fictional EP Tracklist

## 1. Terminal Nine Hears the Second String

**Story & Context:** At 130 BPM, Aigerim opens with one repeated downstroke figure where the lower string speaks first and the upper string follows a fraction later. Marcus places kick on ATTACK, leaves CROSS open for four cycles, then introduces one clipped rim exactly in that gap. Dana adds a single prepared-clavichord fifth after every second CROSS. Timur remains silent until bar sixteen, then uses one muted frame-drum center hit after SNAP. Nora enters only when the mono bass disappears for two bars. In the central section Aigerim reverses the hand direction while every electronic part stays identical, making the groove feel lighter even though tempo and note order remain unchanged.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Telegraph terminal block marked 9, two copper wires entering at slightly different angles, a three-part timing diagram labeled ATTACK / CROSS / SNAP beneath a dombra bridge in shallow focus. Sparse vocal fragments limited to “terminal nine,” “second string,” “same signal,” and “reverse the hand.”

## 2. Relay Four Leaves One Slot Blank

**Story & Context:** At 136 BPM, Marcus builds the EP’s densest electro pattern but every fourth dombra phrase triggers a full Relay Gap after SNAP. No hat, clap, bass, clavichord, or percussion fills that subdivision. Aigerim alternates rapid two-string cells with muted ghost strokes. Dana plays short suspended-second ticks only in CROSS. Timur restarts selected cycles with one sharp rim stroke after the blank slot. Nora supplies short low fundamentals during the largest gaps. The track’s tension comes from hearing a highly active groove repeatedly refuse one expected event.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Relay cabinet numbered 4 with one empty socket in an otherwise complete row, a waveform stopping cleanly before the next impact, snow-lit window beyond. Sparse vocal fragments limited to “relay four,” “leave one blank,” “do not fill,” and “send again.”

## 3. Winter Circuit Returns Upstroke First

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 124 BPM and begins with almost no hats. Aigerim plays an upstroke-led figure so the upper string speaks first, giving the dombra an unusually light front edge. Marcus answers SNAP with short mono-bass notes every two bars. Dana reduces her clavichord part to isolated octave taps. Timur uses one low dabyl hit only at major section changes. Nora holds soft chalumeau notes beneath sub-free passages. In the final minute the same phrase gradually transitions from upstroke-led to downstroke-led without changing melody. The rhythm feels heavier cycle by cycle even though the machine pattern is unchanged. The final sound is one dry bridge SNAP followed by silence.

